 ## Background
-Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) originated in computational fluid dynamics to efficiently resolve multiscale phenomena. The Berger-Colella (BC) AMR framework is a foundational standard for block-structured AMR, where grids are nested hierarchically [S2, unofficial]. Early visualization efforts, such as those by Kähler and Hege, extended tree-based techniques like 3DTM to render large and sparse AMR datasets by partitioning the volume into axis-aligned bricks [S1]. Subsequent developments addressed the "stitching" problem at resolution boundaries, where cell-centered data on different levels does not align, leading to artifacts like "ghosting" or cracks [S2, unofficial][S3]. Methods evolved from simple nearest-neighbor lookups to continuous basis function reconstructions and exact geometric rendering techniques that preserve mass conservation [S1][S2, unofficial]. Modern implementations often utilize Space-Filling Curves (SFC) for cache-efficient storage of block-structured data [S1] and integrate with parallel visualization frameworks like VisIt and OSPRay [S2, S4, unofficial].
+Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) originated in computational fluid dynamics to efficiently resolve multiscale phenomena [G]. The Berger-Colella (BC) AMR framework is a foundational standard for block-structured AMR, where grids are nested hierarchically [S2, unofficial]. Early visualization efforts, such as those by Kähler and Hege, extended tree-based techniques like 3DTM to render large and sparse AMR datasets by partitioning the volume into axis-aligned bricks [S1]. Subsequent developments addressed the "stitching" problem at resolution boundaries, where cell-centered data on different levels does not align, leading to artifacts like "ghosting" or cracks [S2, unofficial][S3]. Methods evolved from simple nearest-neighbor lookups to continuous basis function reconstructions and exact geometric rendering techniques that preserve mass conservation [S2, unofficial]. Modern implementations often utilize Space-Filling Curves (SFC) for cache-efficient storage of block-structured data [S1] and integrate with parallel visualization frameworks like VisIt [S4, unofficial] and OSPRay [S2, unofficial].
 
